INTERPRETATION

LU-2F remains FALSE.

The absolute legacy gate was preregistered
and cannot be removed after seeing results.

However the failure does not support
a conclusion that U_A25 degraded legacy clearance.

The exact U_A0 parent itself
does not achieve 95-percent legacy clearance
in 3 of the 10 fresh worlds.

The low-dose arm:

matches the parent exactly in two of those worlds;

and differs by only about half a percentage point
in the third.

All comparative preservation gates pass.

Therefore the failed absolute gate
is baseline-infeasible for this Task-1 horizon
in a nontrivial fraction of fresh worlds.

This is a measurement-contract problem.

It is not a scientific pass.

It is also not evidence
of a learned-U capacity regression.

DECISION

Do not promote U_A25.

Do not retroactively remove the 0.95 gate.

Do not tune alpha.

Record the baseline infeasibility explicitly.

The next research step should not be
another attempt to rescue U_A25
with progressively rewritten Task-1 gates.

The Task-1 evidence now says:

25-percent learned U authority
causally changes development;

pairwise function is highly preserved;

internal trajectories are path-sensitive;

but absolute horizon/window metrics
are themselves unstable or infeasible
for the exact parent in some fresh worlds.

NEXT JUSTIFIED DIRECTION

Move away from repeated Task-1 metric surgery.

Use the accumulated Task-1 lineage
as evidence that bounded learned local control
is viable enough to test on
a second task environment
with its own preregistered,
baseline-feasible evaluation contract.

Do not call U_A25 promoted.

Do not alter Developmental Substrate V0.2.

Instead ask whether the learned H/U capabilities
generalize outside the Task-1 workload
before spending more experiments
optimizing Task-1-specific authority gates.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

The neural recruiter did what we wanted
relative to the hand-written organism.

It kept up with new work.

It cleared old work at essentially the same rate.

It controlled backlog.

It preserved recovery.

It preserved safety.

It changed 44 real recruitment decisions.

But one absolute rule said
both versions must clear at least 95 percent
of old work.

In three worlds,
even the hand-written organism could not do that
within the fixed time horizon.

So the experiment still fails,
because that was the rule we wrote.

But that failure is not telling us
the neural recruiter was worse.

It is telling us
we have squeezed about as much useful information
as we should from this one Task-1 scoring setup.

The better next move is to see
whether the learned local behavior
works in a different task environment.

